#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055706} :activity/id #uuid "d981772b-339b-55c1-975c-c5ef7ec6cb31" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291 :topic/id #uuid "3d5ca35e-6400-55ca-b230-0ff03628b370" :topic/math-academy-id 112} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055959 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in the first quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/bc/bca60509a15e4d32d06d96c7cf5eac65a00b4e5cb72242152dd998b933209d7d.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $P$ coincides with the cosine of the angle $\\theta,$ and\n\n- the $y$-coordinate coincides with the sine of the angle $\\theta.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nConsequently, if we know the angle $\\theta,$ we can easily work out the coordinates of the point $P,$ and vice-versa. This works regardless of whether the angle $\\theta$ is given in degrees or radians.\n\nFor now, we'll focus on points located in the first quadrant. Later, we will encounter points in all quadrants." :tutorial/id #uuid "8e9912a6-a3c6-5076-9724-a53f5e215dd8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2493 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "03f7f3cb-7a44-54e1-965c-a670919e31f2" :step/math-academy-id 453943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054514 :question/id #uuid "c4d9963b-1fed-523f-89c7-961ffb12feee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b2f3c7-1600-51c6-91ad-855bcee7d18a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535067}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle Given Its Central Angle"} :step/id #uuid "38d038fc-1d12-524b-b9d7-96c6225df167" :step/math-academy-id 453944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055960 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the unit circle and a point $P(x,y)$ that lies on the circle.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nTo prove that $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta,$ let's examine the triangle that's formed by $\\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/53/53d84bedcddcb484bc613a2f01e002afe16b614daa909ff744195bcc45464472.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that: \n\n- The hypotenuse has length $1$ since it is a radius of the unit circle.\n\n- The adjacent and opposite sides have lengths $x$ and $y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, using the definitions of $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{1} = x, \\qquad  \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{1} = y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e7838544-050b-5270-a16a-1679e3489a02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2494 :tutorial/title "A Proof of the Unit Circle Property"} :step/id #uuid "8eef6615-9b8a-526e-9e9e-25070edfdad2" :step/math-academy-id 453948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054515 :question/id #uuid "59696ddf-7aa4-561a-ac77-2216038294b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3346"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "568011de-56c0-55f4-95ec-0a426cbf6b67" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049794}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534980}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Central Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "9d51bf5e-1d13-5f8d-9fd6-c02b852842c4" :step/math-academy-id 453949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054517 :question/id #uuid "acb093c8-daa1-5eee-8678-e985fb9dfd71" :question/math-academy-id "e-3348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8487f45b-a734-5375-abc2-5a347d49587c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551269}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "a21ae939-d688-510c-9d02-aec18c105046" :step/math-academy-id 453952 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055963 :tutorial/content "To find the tangent of an angle using the unit circle, we need to use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let's take a look at the following triangle.\n\n![](images/99/99b51d7d25a266dbfda7839be6bafc335b181ecd6dc74224fcb0557180f872aa.png)\n\nFor this particular triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{z}, \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{z}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nWhen we take $\\sin\\theta$ and divide it by $\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{y}{z}÷\\frac{x}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{z}\\cdot \\frac{z}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot z}{x\\cdot z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\cancel{z}}{x\\cdot \\cancel{z}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough this proof is only for acute angles, the identity is true for any angle, provided that $\\tan\\theta$ is well-defined.\n\nFinally, since $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta},$ it immediately follows that\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a17ae159-de66-547d-8e9a-2190b512ab3c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2725 :tutorial/title "The Relationship Between Sine, Cosine, and Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "68735c4a-f7f9-5265-80af-ded103195f00" :step/math-academy-id 453955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054516 :question/id #uuid "ee188df6-0ef5-5819-8fd9-77d48e8a5dce" :question/math-academy-id "e-3347"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a4297b-016b-5182-bf4f-cd2b64a23c41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551277}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent or Cotangent of a Central Angle Given a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "8e354a30-502e-5775-810b-ba93046cedfb" :step/math-academy-id 453956}] :activity/title "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054870}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551372} :activity/id #uuid "415fc07e-39c3-5797-979e-ef79737b49e5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291 :topic/id #uuid "3d5ca35e-6400-55ca-b230-0ff03628b370" :topic/math-academy-id 112} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d65c081f-46e7-50d2-b0af-c99aac626906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550948} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1767cf0-549a-5130-876d-9403ed25774d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44100483-c880-5325-8cb2-5b4178bd1ae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-267389" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe2f1266db9f675a2c82803728d37f53d0995c25cf61f74304a731c3d21c64c8.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\frac{5}{6})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/489a919487ffe7051a27f736ff8ca210e4c246bceac42055e31f5150bc4e094b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1263712a-3a0a-57fc-89c3-d4db066a7847" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b0de47-6d93-56f4-9eec-48894f36d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550384} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4fe516-3675-584c-b3d4-a4182956369b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550503} {:answer/id #uuid "72ba0ba6-9d3d-570e-859e-0393addd7b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550656} {:answer/id #uuid "7400282a-889c-5443-9fca-18dc0075e6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550659} {:answer/id #uuid "f948c330-b81c-5ef2-94de-f3c68097c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{13}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550384} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c3804a-ba42-5eda-8d70-9caba3ae75d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "29930a9c-51d8-56d8-87b6-15535f5105d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124392" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e597e317f32a2a9a3757ce583a2b053f4cba1da1a2119c572671e7edc96c61e4.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{6}{7},\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})}{(\\frac{6}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d9f8358-1b2c-5a6e-9744-40ad486a7663" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b60d51-d24a-5296-b05c-ca2de01453fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550944} :answer-field/id #uuid "9194ee95-2b5a-5c6e-af69-3efb185447ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5237ac2b-249d-5de5-a0f0-312a03200ea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-267419" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8fc1b09d51bf0d7ac526a917b2341015b787b3098627bedd25e1638ecf041cc.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fd/fdddfbe8431756e0e0341ad6576383764eb88519cc0454280ce4d443559d3536.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b2579eff-0975-5a6a-82fa-36ae232133c1"}] :activity/title "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550345}]}
